# (WIP) Clothing consumption calculations/sources
# 
# [1] https://publications.gc.ca/collections/collection_2018/statcan/34-216/CS34-216-1960-eng.pdf
# [2] https://publications.gc.ca/collections/collection_2018/statcan/34-217/CS34-217-1960-eng.pdf
# Canadian detailed statistics on garment industry, 1960 (men's and women's clothing, separately)
# [3] https://publications.gc.ca/collections/collection_2019/isde-ised/id46/Id46-2-1980-1-eng.pdf
# Canadian gov't inquiry on garment industry, 1980
# These sources are very useful as they give data on both dollar value of garments sold in Canada as well as, in the case of the 1980 report, piece counts.
# Historical data in the 1960 report goes back to 1929 which is the earliest the Canadian govt appears to have collected this information.
# 
# [4] https://transportgeography.org/contents/chapter5/intermodal-transportation-containerization/container-shipping-costs-value/
# Actual textbook, not AI slop, giving piece counts and dollar values of a 40' shipping container full of clothes.
# This allows us to convert piece count into approximate mass, and since we can convert dollars into piece count,
# we can therefore directly convert historical economic data on garment purchases in dollars into "how many crates of textiles does this mean".
# 
# [5] https://www.statista.com/statistics/294277/clothing-number-of-retail-stores-in-the-united-kingdom-uk/
# Source for figure of 11,500 clothing stores in the UK today 
# 
# Assumptions (getting figures that roughly fit in an order of magnitude of Simutrans scale is very difficult otherwise)
# 1. Using figures for Canada will be roughly comparable to British figures, at least in the 20th century
# 2. A 40' container of clothes has a ~20,000kg payload
# 3. Of a 440kg crate of textiles let's say 90% is actual textiles (400kg payload per crate)
# 4. A 40' crate has anywhere from 25-130k units of clothes in it, so let's say 75k
# 5. For department stores, assume 50% floor space is for clothing and that they have two floors, so the 1x3 footprint is effectively 2x3 * 50% = 1x3 = 3 tiles of clothing store
# 6. Over time there is a similar ratio of clothing stores to population as today
# 7. A typical clothing store is around ~2000 square feet or 180 square meters
# 8. GIGANTIC ASSUMPTION: the price of an article of clothing is relatively static, adjusted for inflation, over the course of the 20th century
# 
# This math also applies to department stores. Each department store counts as effectively 3 clothing stores (see assumption 5),
# so depending on distribution_weight of department stores clothing stores will have their consumption reduced to (department store distribution_weight)/(department store distribution_weight + 3)
# e.g. if distribution weight of a department store is 7, then clothing stores have their consumption reduced to 7/10 of what it would be ordinarily.
# 
# WIP MATH TO TIDY UP LATER:
# - 24,201,544 population canada
# - 56,284,863 population uk
# - 11500 clothing shops today in UK * 56284863 / 70mn = 9246 shops
#
# Apparent CA market for clothing, 1979: 535.7m "units"
# - a 40ft container has anywhere from 25k to 130k units in it -> assume 75k units
# - 20000kg / 75k units = 0.26kg per unit
# - 0.26kg * 535.7 = 142.85 mn kg
# - correct to UK population: 332 mn kg
# 332 mn kg / 9246 shops = 35,932 kg/shop/year
# * 86 shops per tile / 365 / 8 * 6.4 = 6772 kg/simutrans shop/simutrans month
# = **16.9 crates per simutrans month in a UK shop in 1980!!**
# What about back in time?
#
# 1979 est. wholesale price: $9.31 domestic, $8.46 import
#- $9.31 * 368.2mn net domestic + $8.46 * 167.5 import = $4,844,992,000 market
# INFLATION FROM 1960 TO 1979: 2.57x
# 1960 market size: 
# - men's domestic: $261,278,917 1960 dollars
#	- exports: $2,672,023 -> net $258,606,894
# - men's imports: $32,476,314 -> TOTAL MEN'S $291,083,208
# - women's domestic: $342,360,243
#	- exports: $3,298,630 -> net $339,061,613
# - women's imports: $79,351,776 -> TOTAL WOMEN'S $418,413,389
# TOTAL 1960 MARKET: $709,496,597 * 2.57 = $1,823,406,254 in 1979 dollars
# THEREFORE from 1960 to 1980 the market increased in size by 
# 4,884,992,000 / 1,823,406,254 = 2.67x
# Canadian population went from 17,898,000 to 24,201,000 -> 1.35x
# Approx. increase: 1.97x relative to population
# - MEANING: 1960 clothing shop is 8.5 crates.
# but wait.. department stores exist..
# you have DepartmentStore1960 and DepartmentStore1990.. and DepartmentStore1935..
# There are 7x clothingstore1945 for every deptstore1960
# - Let's say a dept. store covers 3x as many sales as a clothing store
# - That means clothing shop * 0.7 = 5.95 crates/mo and dept store = 17.85 crates
# By DeptStore1990.. let's say we also have a ClothingShop1980..
# Dist. weight of 5, so 5x clothingstore1980 for every deptstore1960..
# - 16.9 * 5/6 = 14.1 crates/mo clothingstore1980
# - 14.1 * 3 = 42 crates/mo deptstore1990
#	
# RESULTS SO FAR ON CLOTHING CONSUMPTION:
# For later-year clothing stores: 14.1 crates per month (TODO: Add ClothesShop1980 for this)
# For later-year department stores, 42 crates/month
# For ClothesShop1945, 6 crates/month
# For department stores in 1960, 17.9 crates/month
# Unknown for ClothesShop1910 or earlier.

